    DIGITAL STATEMENT ABOUT NEW 64-BIT UNIX PROGRAMMING STANDARDS


              Digital's Leadership in 64-Bit Computing
                 Validated by New Industry Standards


SAN FRANCISCO -- February 14, 1996 -- Digital fully supports the 
64-bit working groups proposal for extending the Single UNIX(r) 
Specification to encompass 64-bit programming environments, and its 
64-bit programming model, LP64, as unveiled today at UniForum. In 
fact, today's Digital UNIX already conforms to these new 64-bit 
standards--and has been providing the associated benefits to users for
the past three years. 

     The LP64 programming model, now part of the new standard, has been 
the basis of Digital UNIX since March, 1993, which now provides Digital
and its customers with a significant competitive advantage. Because of
its leadership position and experience in 64-bit UNIX computing,
Digital was an active participant in the working group's efforts to
develop 64-bit standards for the industry. The investment made in
Digital UNIX by Digital's customers, systems integrators and software
developers is now not only fully protected, but further enhanced, as
the rest of the industry strives to meet the new standards.

     "These new industry standards for 64-bit programming provide the 
industry with a common roadmap for the future," said Don Harbert, vice
president, UNIX Business Segment, Digital Equipment Corp. "By 
standardizing on Digital's approach and data model for 64-bit, the 
industry is clearly endorsing the significance of Digitals strategy to
its customers and the marketplace. Over the next few years, the rest
of the industry will be able to reap the benefits of 64-bit computing
already available to users of Digitals 64-bit Alpha hardware and
software, including unsurpassed application and database performance
through the ability to address Very Large Memory (VLM64) and very large
file systems. These new standards serve to reinforce Digital's
long-held beliefs in the value and benefits of 64-bit computing and
validate the efforts and technology expertise with 64-bit UNIX that
Digital has already brought to the industry, and for which it has set
the standard."
