        PROVEN CAPABILITIES OF DIGITAL'S ALPHA ARCHITECTURE 
               TO SUPPORT ORACLE (R) UNIVERSAL SERVER (R)


       ...Alpha technology provides customers with a powerful
 engine to optimize data manipulation across multiple data types...


SAN FRANCISCO, Calif., February 26, 1996 -- Taking another step 
forward in its long-standing strategic relationship with Oracle 
Corporation, of Redwood Shores, California., Digital Equipment 
Corporation today announced that its family of AlphaServer 8000 
systems will play a pivotal role in supporting Oracles's Universal 
Server strategy.

     Oracle Universal Server is a complete information management 
solution for companies seeking to capitalize on the advantages of 
accessing any type of data; relational, text, or multimedia, from 
any application, over any network.  The proven performance 
capabilities of Digital's Very Large Memory (VLM64)-enhanced 
AlphaServer systems, working with Oracle's 64-bit option in large 
and demanding application environments, will provide users with a 
solution that is powerful, cost-effective and comprehensive. 

     Digital's 64-bit systems are recognized as an ideal platform 
for customers who are managing large amounts of complex data. 
Oracle was the first database vendor to take full advantage of 
Digital's 64-bit environment. In December 1995, Oracle unveiled a 
string of record-breaking single-system benchmarks using an 
AlphaServer 8400 5/350 system and Oracle7 with the Oracle 64-bit 
option. The benchmark results utilized the industry-standard TPC-C 
test. 

     "Digital's 64-bit Alpha architecture, teamed with Oracle's 
VLM64 database technology puts both companies in the position of 
being able to deliver a technology solution to customers that is 
cost-effective, and is on the cutting-edge," said Mike Gallup, 
Digital's Vice President of Worldwide Systems Marketing. "Today, the 
Alpha architecture stands alone in its unique ability to manage and 
manipulate huge amounts of multiple data better than any technology 
in the industry, and Digital is delighted to embrace and fully 
support Oracle's Universal Server strategy."   

     "Digital and Oracle are market leaders in providing a complete 
64-bit solution, including 64-bit hardware, operating systems, very 
large memory databases, and applications. This leadership in 64-bit 
technology enables us to offer open systems customers the world's 
fastest enterprise-class database performance," said Robert 
Pariseau, Vice President of Oracle's Digital Products Division.
"With our record-setting benchmarks and multi-Terabyte sized data 
warehouse solutions, we have proven that Oracle's Universal Server 
continues to scale exceptionally well on Digital's AlphaServer 
platforms, providing our customers superior power and reliability 
for their most demanding environment."

     The business/technology partnership between Digital and Oracle 
has been a long and productive one. As far back as 1993 both 
companies realized that customers were rapidly approaching the 
limits of conventional 32-bit computing systems, and began 
collaborative development efforts on database software in order to 
take full advantage of the VLM64 technology, based on Digital's 
64-bit Alpha architecture. In April of 1995 Oracle became the first 
database company to offer customers VLM technology for 64-bit 
systems. 

     The key role that Digital's Alpha platform will play in 
supporting Oracle's Universal Server strategy represents another 
significant step forward in the productive partnership that exists 
between the two companies.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computers to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's Intel and Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
